---
title: Imaging
order: 6
description: Disk cloning, imaging with dd, partclone, and ISO writing
tags: [disk, dd, partclone, clone, imaging, iso]
---

Tools for low-level disk and partition copying, backup images, and writing bootable media.

@text
dd — byte-level copy. Powerful but unforgiving. Double-check device names.

@code bash
# Clone entire disk to another disk
sudo dd if=/dev/sda of=/dev/sdb bs=64K status=progress conv=fsync

# Create a compressed disk image
sudo dd if=/dev/sda bs=64K status=progress | gzip > disk.img.gz

# Restore compressed image to disk
gunzip -c disk.img.gz | sudo dd of=/dev/sda bs=64K status=progress conv=fsync

# Clone a single partition
sudo dd if=/dev/sda1 of=partition.img bs=64K status=progress

# Write an ISO to USB drive
sudo dd if=archlinux.iso of=/dev/sdb bs=4M status=progress conv=fsync

# Create a file full of zeros (for sparse file testing, swap, etc.)
dd if=/dev/zero of=swapfile bs=1M count=4096 status=progress

# Wipe first 1 MiB of a disk (destroy partition table)
sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda bs=1M count=1

# Read MBR (first 512 bytes)
sudo dd if=/dev/sda of=mbr.bin bs=512 count=1

# Key options:
#   bs=SIZE        block size (larger = faster, 4M-64K typical)
#   count=N        copy only N blocks
#   status=progress  show transfer progress
#   conv=fsync     flush data before finishing
#   conv=noerror,sync  continue on read errors, pad with zeros
#   iflag=direct   bypass page cache for reads
#   oflag=direct   bypass page cache for writes

@text
Safer ISO writing with cp or cat (simpler than dd for ISOs).

@code bash
# Write ISO with cp (handles block size automatically)
sudo cp archlinux.iso /dev/sdb
sudo sync

# Or with cat
sudo cat archlinux.iso > /dev/sdb
sudo sync

@text
partclone — filesystem-aware cloning (faster than dd, skips free space).

@code bash
# Install partclone
sudo pacman -S partclone

# Clone ext4 partition to image
sudo partclone.ext4 -c -s /dev/sda1 -o sda1.img

# Clone with compression
sudo partclone.ext4 -c -s /dev/sda1 | gzip > sda1.img.gz

# Restore from image
sudo partclone.ext4 -r -s sda1.img -o /dev/sda1

# Restore compressed image
gunzip -c sda1.img.gz | sudo partclone.ext4 -r -o /dev/sda1

# Clone btrfs partition
sudo partclone.btrfs -c -s /dev/sda1 -o sda1.btrfs.img

# Clone FAT32 (EFI partition)
sudo partclone.fat32 -c -s /dev/sda1 -o efi.img

# Filesystem-agnostic clone (copies all blocks like dd but with progress)
sudo partclone.dd -s /dev/sda1 -o sda1.img

@text
Verify and check images.

@code bash
# Verify a dd image matches the source
sudo dd if=/dev/sda1 bs=64K | md5sum
md5sum partition.img

# Generate checksum for an image
sha256sum disk.img.gz > disk.img.gz.sha256

# Verify checksum
sha256sum -c disk.img.gz.sha256

# Verify ISO integrity before writing
sha256sum archlinux.iso

@text
Network cloning — clone over SSH.

@code bash
# Clone disk to a remote machine
sudo dd if=/dev/sda bs=64K status=progress | ssh user@remote "dd of=/dev/sda bs=64K"

# Clone with compression over network
sudo dd if=/dev/sda bs=64K | gzip | ssh user@remote "gunzip | dd of=/dev/sda bs=64K"

# Pull a remote disk image
ssh user@remote "sudo dd if=/dev/sda bs=64K" | dd of=remote-disk.img bs=64K status=progress
